body='''
<section class="guide-start" aria-labelledby="before"><h2 id="before">Before you start</h2><p>This guide is for a business owner using the current FaraiOS workspace. Sign in, create your business and check its currency, time zone, services and staff records. Use a business you own. Do not practise with real customer records or send example links to customers.</p><p>The example below follows a fictional cleaning enquiry, but the same sequence can support other service businesses. It is a worked example, not a customer testimonial or a promise that every specialist workflow is available.</p><div class="example"><p class="eyebrow">Fictional example</p><h3>One customer. One agreed job.</h3><p>Example Customer asks for a one-off property clean. After clarifying the work, the business quotes <strong>R850</strong>, schedules a <strong>180-minute visit</strong> in <strong>Africa/Johannesburg</strong>, assigns available staff and issues an invoice. The amount and duration are teaching examples, not FaraiOS subscription pricing or a recommended cleaning price.</p></div></section>
<nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="Walkthrough contents"><h2>In this guide</h2><ol><li><a href="#review-enquiry">Review the enquiry</a></li><li><a href="#prepare-quote">Prepare and share a quote</a></li><li><a href="#customer-acceptance">Wait for customer acceptance</a></li><li><a href="#schedule-job">Schedule and assign the job</a></li><li><a href="#issue-invoice">Create and issue the invoice</a></li><li><a href="#record-payment">Record a payment received</a></li><li><a href="#handover-checklist">Use the handover checklist</a></li></ol></nav>
<div class="guide-steps" aria-label="Workflow overview"><span>Enquiry<br><small>Clarify the request</small></span><span>Quote<br><small>Agree the scope</small></span><span>Scheduled job<br><small>Plan the work</small></span><span>Invoice<br><small>Record what is due</small></span></div>
<article class="guide-article">
<section id="review-enquiry"><p class="eyebrow">Step 01</p><h2>Review the enquiry before quoting</h2><p>Open <strong>Enquiries</strong> and select the customer request. Check the service, requirements and contact details. If you received the request by phone or another channel, use <strong>Create enquiry</strong> to record it in your business workspace.</p><p>Clarify anything that could change the work: the property or site, service area, access arrangements, preferred time and what is included or excluded. Keep confidential access codes out of public pages and ordinary notes.</p><p>Set the enquiry to <strong>Contacted</strong> once you have actually contacted the customer. Add private notes and a follow-up date, then choose <strong>Save enquiry</strong>. Private notes are for your business; they are not copied into customer quotes or emails.</p><aside class="guide-note"><strong>Example:</strong> confirm whether the requested clean includes interior windows. Record the agreed scope before setting a price. An enquiry is a request, not a confirmed job.</aside></section>
<section id="prepare-quote"><p class="eyebrow">Step 02</p><h2>Prepare a quote the customer can understand</h2><p>From the enquiry, choose <strong>Create quote</strong>. Check the carried-over customer details and requirements. Add the service or item, quantity and unit price, plus a clear scope and a suitable <strong>Valid through</strong> date.</p><div class="example"><h3>Example quote</h3><dl><div><dt>Service</dt><dd>Agreed one-off property clean</dd></div><div><dt>Quantity × unit price</dt><dd>1 × R850</dd></div><div><dt>Total offered</dt><dd>R850</dd></div><div><dt>Scope note</dt><dd>Include only the work agreed with the customer; list exclusions clearly.</dd></div></dl></div><p>Choose <strong>Save draft</strong>. Review the saved quote, not just the values still typed in the form. When it is ready, confirm the saved quote is ready to share and select <strong>Mark sent &amp; get link</strong>. Marking it sent locks those saved details.</p><p>Copy the customer link and share it through your usual agreed communication channel. <strong>Marking a quote sent does not automatically email it.</strong> Anyone with the link can view the quote, so share it only with the intended customer.</p></section>
<section id="customer-acceptance"><p class="eyebrow">Step 03</p><h2>Check that the customer has accepted</h2><p>The customer reviews the quote through its customer link and can accept or decline it. In your workspace, check that the status is <strong>Accepted</strong> before using the quote to create a booking.</p><p>If the quote is declined or has expired, discuss the requirements again and prepare the appropriate next quote. Do not treat a sent quote, an enquiry acknowledgement or silence as acceptance.</p><aside class="guide-note"><strong>Important distinction:</strong> accepting the quote agrees its recorded scope and price. You still need to arrange the actual visit with the customer and check capacity before scheduling it.</aside></section>
<section id="schedule-job"><p class="eyebrow">Step 04</p><h2>Schedule the agreed job and assign staff</h2><p>Open the accepted quote and select <strong>Create / open booking</strong>. Check the preserved agreed details. Enter the service address, date, start time and duration, then choose <strong>Save booking</strong>.</p><p>Read the time zone shown beside the schedule. In this example, a 09:00 start and 180-minute duration means planning a three-hour visit in Africa/Johannesburg. Confirm the actual visit arrangements with the customer through your agreed channel.</p><p>In <strong>Assigned staff</strong>, select the relevant staff records and choose <strong>Save assignments</strong>. Booking details and assignments are saved separately: save schedule edits first, then assignments.</p><p>Use the booking list or <strong>Calendar</strong> view to check the schedule. FaraiOS allows overlapping bookings, so an overlap warning is a prompt to check capacity, not a guarantee that a slot has been blocked. Staff assignment does not by itself give that person a login or send them a message.</p><aside class="guide-note"><strong>After the visit:</strong> update the booking status to reflect what actually happened. Status changes are manual; a scheduled date passing does not prove a job was completed. Keep cancellations in the record rather than treating them as completed work.</aside></section>
<section id="issue-invoice"><p class="eyebrow">Step 05</p><h2>Create the invoice from the booking</h2><p>Open the saved, non-cancelled booking and choose <strong>Create / open invoice</strong>. This keeps the invoice connected to the job and preserves its agreed customer and item details. If an invoice already exists for that booking, the interface opens it.</p><p>Check the customer, items, currency and total. Set <strong>Due on</strong> to the date agreed with the customer, then choose <strong>Save draft</strong>. In the worked example, the invoice carries the agreed R850; it is not a new opportunity to silently change the price.</p><p>Review the saved invoice and confirm that it is ready. Select <strong>Issue invoice &amp; get link</strong>. Issuing locks the saved details and creates the issued document. Copy its customer link and share it only with the intended customer. <strong>No invoice email is sent automatically by this action.</strong></p><p>If issued information needs correcting, use the supported correction process and retain the history. Do not alter or delete records merely to make the figures look right.</p></section>
<section id="record-payment"><p class="eyebrow">Step 06</p><h2>Record money you have actually received</h2><p>FaraiOS can record a payment received outside the platform. Open the invoice and record the amount, payment date and a useful reference. Check the details and the confirmation before saving.</p><div class="example"><h3>Example balance</h3><dl><div><dt>Issued invoice</dt><dd>R850</dd></div><div><dt>Payment received and recorded</dt><dd>R300</dd></div><div><dt>Remaining balance</dt><dd>R550 · Part Paid</dd></div><div><dt>After a further R550 is received and recorded</dt><dd>R0 remaining · Paid</dd></div></dl></div><p>Recording a payment does not charge a card, move money or prove that funds cleared. Check your actual payment records first. Likewise, reversing a payment record corrects the ledger; it does not send a refund.</p></section>
<section id="handover-checklist"><p class="eyebrow">A reusable checklist</p><h2>Check the record before the next handover</h2><ul class="guide-checklist"><li><strong>Enquiry → quote:</strong> correct customer, clear requirements, agreed scope, contact method and follow-up date.</li><li><strong>Quote → booking:</strong> accepted quote, unchanged agreed details, correct address and confirmed visit arrangements.</li><li><strong>Booking → delivery:</strong> correct date, time zone and duration; capacity checked; staff assignments saved separately.</li><li><strong>Booking → invoice:</strong> correct linked job, customer, currency, items, amount and due date; saved draft reviewed before issue.</li><li><strong>Invoice → payment record:</strong> funds actually received; amount, date and reference checked; remaining balance reconciled.</li></ul><p>You can use this checklist for your team’s handovers without sharing customer links or private records publicly.</p></section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Common questions</h2><details><summary>Is this workflow automatic?</summary><p>No. This guide describes an owner-managed workflow. Customer quote acceptance changes the quote status, but the owner still schedules the job, saves assignments, issues the invoice and records payments.</p></details><details><summary>Does a quote or invoice link require care?</summary><p>Yes. Anyone with that link can view its document. Do not put real customer links in public case studies, screenshots, directory listings or social posts.</p></details><details><summary>Can another industry follow these steps?</summary><p>The enquiry, quote, booking and invoice sequence is shared. Confirm any specialist needs, such as treatment-room capacity, compliance certificates, memberships or recurring billing, before relying on the platform.</p></details><details><summary>How much does FaraiOS cost?</summary><p>FaraiOS is currently free to use in its prototype stage. The example service prices in this guide are fictional amounts charged by an example business, not software fees. See the <a href="../pricing">pricing page</a> for current information.</p></details></section>
<section class="guide-end"><h2>Try it with your own process</h2><p>Start with one clearly scoped enquiry. Check the record at every handover and keep customer communication deliberate. For help with the current workflow, <a href="../contact.html">contact FaraiOS</a>. You can also <a href="../features">explore the current features</a> or <a href="../account/index.php?page=signup">create an account</a>.</p><p class="guide-meta">Written by FaraiOS · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-15">15 November 2026</time> · Based on the current owner workspace. Examples are fictional and do not create records or send messages.</p></section>
</article>'''
